Subject-perspective review complete and saved to `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Demis-Hassabis/2026-08-29_021004/subject.md`.

**Verdict:** trust strained, value high, recommendation **revise** — 1 blocker, 7 concerns. Hassabis would recognize himself in this piece; the fairness architecture (his own verified words as spine, privacy respected, responsibility kept on the weapons-pledge repeal, his stated step-down reason treated as the primary account) is genuinely strong.

**The blocker (SUBJECT-R1):** the Liechtenstein origin scene narrates contested particulars as established fact. My research resolved this cleanly — I fetched Hassabis's own first-person introduction to *Game Changer* and confirmed every detail ("veteran Danish master," age eleven, eight hours, the resignation and gestured stalemate save) comes faithfully from **his telling**, while Althöfer's 2024 chess-history investigation identifies the opponent as a ~25-year-old German FIDE-Master at the 1989 Liechtenstein Open. The repair is one attributive clause framing the scene as his own telling — which is also fairer to him than omniscient narration, since it protects him from looking like a fabulist if the correction circulates.

**Strongest hit:** the empathy turn — "They are one worry, pointed in two directions" — which explains the doomsaying-while-racing charge through his own verified vocabulary without excusing him.

**Highest-priority concern:** the two Mallaby marquee quotes (the "tanks on the lawn" pull quote and Silver's "Jedi-level charisma") ride a single reviewer's transcription of a book nobody in the pipeline has opened; I recommend converting the draft's own promised book-check into a listed publish blocker. Runner-up: the "estranged" Suleyman characterization is attributed to Mallaby's book but sourced only to publisher marketing copy.
